---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps in the browser — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightCraft is another wonderful Lightroom replacement written in Rust that runs in a browser tab. Here is what I learned when I put it next to LightPhotos. I include some measurements, but they are not a fully equal comparison."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps in the browser</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft">LightCraft</a>
          is another wonderful Lightroom replacement, written in Rust, that
          runs in a browser tab. Here is what I learned when I put it next to
          LightPhotos. I include some measurements, but they are not a fully
          equal comparison.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Both apps are Rust and egui compiled to WebAssembly, with downloads
          of 3.5&nbsp;MB and 3.8&nbsp;MB compressed. On twenty Sony RAW
          files, LightPhotos filled its grid in 1.4&nbsp;s and showed each
          photo in 0.53&nbsp;s. LightCraft took 2.3&nbsp;s and 1.88&nbsp;s,
          and used nearly twice the memory. Most of the gap is the starting
          point. At fit-to-window, LightPhotos edits the JPEG that the camera
          already made. LightCraft develops the RAW data itself every time.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="start-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-view="fit" aria-pressed="true">Fit to window</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-view="zoom" aria-pressed="false">Zoomed in</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-act="next">Next photo</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 232" role="img" aria-label="What each app starts from. A RAW file holds a full-size JPEG that the camera made, and the sensor data. At fit-to-window, LightPhotos applies your edits to the embedded JPEG and shows the next photo in 0.53 seconds. When you zoom in, it decodes the RAW data. LightCraft develops the sensor data for every view and shows the next photo in 1.88 seconds.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE RAW FILE</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="250" y="12">WORK FOR THE NEXT PHOTO</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="26" width="200" height="180" rx="8"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" data-el="jpeg" x="16" y="40" width="168" height="64" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="32" y="66">Embedded JPEG</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="32" y="86">made by the camera</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="128" width="168" height="64" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="32" y="154">Sensor data</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="32" y="174">needs a full develop</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" data-el="from-jpeg" d="M184 72 H240"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-line" data-el="from-raw" d="M184 150 C214 150 212 72 240 72" opacity="0"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M240 67 L250 72 L240 77 Z"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M184 160 H240"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M240 155 L250 160 L240 165 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" data-el="lp-box" x="250" y="34" width="390" height="76" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="266" y="58">LightPhotos</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" data-el="lp-work" x="266" y="78">Your edits on the camera's JPEG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="266" y="90" width="270" height="8" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" data-el="lp-bar" x="266" y="90" width="76" height="8" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" data-el="lp-time" x="556" y="78">0.53 s</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="250" y="122" width="390" height="76" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="266" y="146">LightCraft</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="266" y="166">Develops the RAW data, then your edits</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="266" y="178" width="270" height="8" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" data-el="lc-bar" x="266" y="178" width="270" height="8" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" data-el="lc-time" x="556" y="166">1.88 s</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="226">Bars run in real time. Median of 8 steps, Chromium, 24-megapixel Sony RAW files.</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">Fit to window: LightPhotos shows the next photo in 0.53 s. LightCraft takes 1.88 s.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Press Next photo to replay one step in real time. Zoomed in,
          LightPhotos decodes the RAW data too.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Same language, different answers</h2>
      <p>
        Both apps are Rust from top to bottom. Both draw their interface
        with egui on a canvas, and each web version is its desktop app
        compiled to WebAssembly. So the language does not decide much. The
        apps still differ on storage, threads, graphics and toolchain, and
        LightPhotos used LightCraft's layout until late September (see <a
        href="/blogs/challenges-with-webassembly.html">challenges with
        WebAssembly</a>).
      </p>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your photos</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Read in place from a folder you pick</td>
              <td>Copied into the browser's own storage on import</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Parallel work</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">6 decode threads that share one memory, with the same queues as the desktop app</td>
              <td>Up to 4 Web Workers, each with its own memory. Jobs go in as JSON, pixels come back</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Graphics</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">WebGPU, the newer API</td>
              <td>WebGL2, which more browsers support</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Browsers</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Chrome, Edge</td>
              <td>No WebGPU or folder access needed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Rust toolchain</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Nightly, standard library rebuilt</td>
              <td>Stable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Server headers</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Required (cross-origin isolation, COOP/COEP)</td>
              <td>Optional</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">What a RAW shows at fit</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">The camera's embedded JPEG, with your edits</td>
              <td>The RAW data, developed, with your edits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">RAW decoder</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">rawler (LGPL-2.1), disclosed with its license</td>
              <td>Its own</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">License</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">MIT or Apache-2.0, plus rawler under LGPL-2.1</td>
              <td>MIT or Apache-2.0</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">After a reload</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Reopen Session continues from where you stopped</td>
              <td>Library is still there</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open in several tabs</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Allowed, with no guard against two tabs that overwrite each other's saves</td>
              <td>One tab at a time</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The Mac app decodes with Apple's ImageIO, so it does not include
        rawler. LightCraft also has many more features. LightPhotos is for
        going through a folder fast, keeping the good photos and giving them
        quick edits. LightCraft is a full Lightroom-style editor, with masks,
        tone curves, colour grading, healing, lens corrections, presets,
        versions, history, HDR and panorama merges, export to six formats,
        and albums, smart albums and stacks.
      </p>

      <h2>How I measured</h2>
      <p>
        I built both apps from their current code and served each from its
        own local address. Playwright drove them in Chromium 154 on an M4 Pro
        Mac with 48&nbsp;GB of memory. The photos were twenty RAW files
        from a Sony A7 II, 24 megapixels and about 25&nbsp;MB each. Neither
        app had seen them before.
      </p>
      <p>
        A timer starts at the key press and stops when the screen stops
        changing: every 150&nbsp;ms the script takes a screenshot, and a step
        is done when three screenshots in a row match. <code>tools/web-bench</code> measures LightPhotos the same
        way, and it works for any app. It is coarse: each number includes
        about 140&nbsp;ms of screenshot overhead. So compare the two apps
        with each other, not with a stopwatch.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Download, compressed (brotli)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">3.5&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>3.8&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">WebAssembly file, uncompressed</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">14.1&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>17.1&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Fill the grid with 20 RAW thumbnails, total</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1.4&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>2.3&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Show the next photo full size, each (median of 8)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.53&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>1.88&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Memory afterwards, all threads and workers</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1.9&nbsp;GB</td>
              <td>3.4&nbsp;GB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        LightCraft includes a whole editing pipeline and its own RAW decoder,
        and it is still only 0.3&nbsp;MB bigger compressed. Its release
        builds also embed a Japanese font, which I left out.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the numbers differ</h2>
      <p>
        At fit-to-window, the camera's embedded JPEG has more pixels than
        the screen can show. So LightPhotos decodes that JPEG and applies
        your sliders to it. Only when you zoom in does it load the full
        decode, which demosaics the sensor data (turns it into a full colour
        image) at full resolution. LightCraft develops the RAW data for every
        view. That is more work, but the colours at fit come from the same
        place as the colours at 100%. It does all that in under two seconds,
        in a browser, on a 24-megapixel file. That is a good result.
      </p>
      <p>
        The grid is closer. The LightPhotos decode threads pull thumbnails
        from one shared queue, and the photo on screen gets priority. When I
        moved to shared memory, the number of thumbnails that decode at once
        went from 1 to 9, because the RAW library had silently run every
        decode on a single fallback thread. LightCraft copies all 498&nbsp;MB into the browser's storage
        while it imports. That copy is part of its 2.3&nbsp;s, and it lets
        the library survive a reload.
      </p>

      <h2>Why LightCraft uses more memory</h2>
      <p>
        Each LightCraft worker is a separate copy of the program with its
        own memory, and WebAssembly memory never shrinks after it grows. The
        main thread also keeps recently used originals, the 498&nbsp;MB of
        RAW files. Four workers plus that come to 3.4&nbsp;GB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shared memory has a cost too. Each LightPhotos decode thread needs
        about 300&nbsp;MB of scratch space for a RAW file, all from one heap
        with a limit of 4&nbsp;GB. With ten threads, memory peaked at
        3.4&nbsp;GB. So I limited the browser build to six threads, which
        gives the 1.9&nbsp;GB.
      </p>

      <h2>The same bug, again</h2>
      <p>
        The first time I imported the RAW files into LightCraft, all four
        workers crashed, and then the page crashed. The console said
        <code>time not implemented on this platform</code>. That is the bug
        from my last post: Rust's standard <code>Instant::now()</code>
        crashes in the browser instead of returning an error.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft already uses a small library that asks the browser's
        clock everywhere else, but one timer in its RAW loader still called
        the standard one. Tests run on the desktop, where that call works,
        and the demo photos are not RAW files, so only a real RAW file
        showed the bug. Swapping in the browser-safe clock is a one-line
        fix. I made it in my local copy, so the LightCraft numbers include
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both apps hit this bug in the same way, so I do not think care is
        the answer. A compiler will not catch it, and a test only catches it
        if it runs in a browser. The better fix is a lint that rejects
        <code>std::time::Instant::now</code> outright, so the mistake cannot
        get in at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        LightPhotos relies on the camera's JPEG, your own folders and shared
        memory, so it feels quick when you go through a shoot. LightCraft
        takes on the whole RAW pipeline and a full Lightroom-style feature
        set, keeps its library in the browser and needs no special server
        configuration. It got all of that working in about a week. I look
        forward to seeing where it goes. Both also run on Mac, Windows and
        Linux, and that comparison is next.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/exporting-to-your-own-immich-server.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Exporting to your own Immich server</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-on-the-mac.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Two Rust photo apps on the Mac</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    // Replays one "next photo" step in real time, at fit or zoomed in.
    const fig = document.getElementById("start-demo");
    if (fig) {
      const el = (name: string) => fig.querySelector(`[data-el="${name}"]`) as SVGElement;
      const readout = fig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout") as HTMLElement;
      const viewBtns = fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-view]");
      const nextBtn = fig.querySelector('[data-act="next"]') as HTMLButtonElement;
      const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");

      const LP = 0.53; // s, LightPhotos at fit (median of 8)
      const LC = 1.88; // s, LightCraft (median of 8)
      const PX_PER_S = 270 / LC;
      let zoom = false;
      let frame = 0;

      const setBar = (name: string, t: number) => el(name).setAttribute("width", String(Math.max(0, t * PX_PER_S)));
      const fmt = (t: number) => `${t.toFixed(2)} s`;

      function draw(t: number) {
        const lc = Math.min(t, LC);
        setBar("lc-bar", lc);
        el("lc-time").textContent = fmt(lc);
        if (zoom) {
          setBar("lp-bar", 0);
          el("lp-time").textContent = "not timed";
        } else {
          const lp = Math.min(t, LP);
          setBar("lp-bar", lp);
          el("lp-time").textContent = fmt(lp);
        }
      }

      function summary() {
        readout.textContent = zoom
          ? "Zoomed in: LightPhotos now decodes the RAW data too. I did not time this case. LightCraft does the same work at every zoom level."
          : "Fit to window: LightPhotos shows the next photo in 0.53 s. LightCraft takes 1.88 s, 1.35 s more.";
      }

      function setView(isZoom: boolean) {
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        zoom = isZoom;
        viewBtns.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String((b.dataset.view === "zoom") === zoom)));
        el("jpeg").setAttribute("class", zoom ? "lp-fig-box" : "lp-fig-box-accent");
        el("from-jpeg").setAttribute("opacity", zoom ? "0" : "1");
        el("from-raw").setAttribute("opacity", zoom ? "1" : "0");
        el("lp-work").textContent = zoom ? "Decodes the RAW data at full size" : "Your edits on the camera's JPEG";
        draw(LC);
        summary();
      }

      function run() {
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        if (reduce.matches) {
          draw(LC);
          summary();
          return;
        }
        readout.textContent = "Next photo…";
        const start = performance.now();
        const tick = (now: number) => {
          const t = Math.max(0, now - start) / 1000;
          draw(t);
          if (t < LC) frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
          else summary();
        };
        frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }

      viewBtns.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener("click", () => setView(b.dataset.view === "zoom")));
      nextBtn.addEventListener("click", run);
    }
  </script>
</Base>
